{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GeomSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_bar_symm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_bar_symm","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L179-L201","doc":"**Bar symmetry in framed form.** `Δ_{T(2,m)}` is palindromic up to sign:\n`q^{m−1} · Δ(q⁻¹) = Δ(q)` unframes to the bar-involution symmetry\n`Δ(q) = Δ(q⁻¹)` for odd `m`. Stated here framed, as\n`∑_{j<m} (−1)^j q^{m−1−j} = ∑_{j<m} (−1)^j q^j` for odd `m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_sub_eq_zero_iff_alexander_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_sub_eq_zero_iff_alexander_root","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L169-L177","doc":"**The vanishing locus is the Alexander root set.** `[n+1]_q = [n]_q`\n(in framed form) exactly when `q` is a root of `Δ_{T(2,2n+1)}`. This is the\nstatement that makes the \"discrete choice instead of fitting\" phrasing in the\n`U_q(su_n)` mass-sum-rule literature a *root-finding* problem rather than a\nparameter fit — an observation about the shape of that programme, not an\nendorsement of its numerics. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_five_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_five_one","kind":"theorem","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L159-L167","doc":"The `T(2,5) = 5₁` instance: `Δ_{5₁}(q) = 1 − q + q² − q³ + q⁴`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_trefoil","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_trefoil","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L149-L157","doc":"**The trefoil instance** (`n = 1`, knot `T(2,3) = 3₁`), the framework's\nelectron knot: `Δ_{3₁}(q) = 1 − q + q²`, and it is the framed difference\n`[2]_q − [1]_q` in framed form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.one_add_mul_framedQInt_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.one_add_mul_framedQInt_sub","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L143-L147","doc":"Combining the two: the framed quantum-integer difference satisfies the\nsame division-free relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.one_add_mul_alexanderTorus2_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.one_add_mul_alexanderTorus2_odd","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L136-L141","doc":"The odd specialisation: `(1 + q) · Δ_{T(2,2n+1)}(q) = 1 + q^{2n+1}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.one_add_mul_alexanderTorus2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.one_add_mul_alexanderTorus2","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L123-L134","doc":"**Division-free closed form.** `(1 + q) · Δ_{T(2,m)}(q) = 1 − (−1)^m q^m`.\nFor odd `m` this is `1 + q^m`, recovering the classical Alexander polynomial\n`Δ_{T(2,m)}(q) = (1 + q^m)/(1 + q)` of the `(2,m)` torus knot without\nrequiring `1 + q` to be invertible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_sub_eq_alexanderTorus2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_sub_eq_alexanderTorus2","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L103-L121","doc":"**The identity.** The framed symmetric quantum-integer difference is the\nnormalized Alexander polynomial of the `(2, 2n+1)` torus knot:\n\n  `q^n · ([n+1]_q − [n]_q) = Δ_{T(2,2n+1)}(q)`.\n\nProved by induction; holds over any commutative ring, for every `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_succ","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L99-L101","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_one","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L96-L97","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2_zero","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L93-L94","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_succ","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L89-L91","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_one","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L86-L87","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt_zero","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L83-L84","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.alexanderTorus2","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L78-L81","doc":"**Normalized Alexander polynomial of the `(2, m)` torus knot**:\n`Δ_{T(2,m)}(q) = ∑_{j<m} (−q)^j`. For odd `m` this is the classical\n`(1 + q^m)/(1 + q)`; see `one_add_mul_alexanderTorus2_odd`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.QIntegerAlexanderTorus.framedQInt","kind":"def","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/QIntegerAlexanderTorus.lean#L72-L76","doc":"**Framed symmetric quantum integer**: `framedQInt q n = ∑_{i<n} q^{2i}`,\nwhich is `q^{n−1} · [n]_q` for the symmetric quantum integer\n`[n]_q = q^{n−1} + ⋯ + q^{−(n−1)}`. Framing clears the negative powers, so\nthe whole file works over a generic `CommRing` (§7c). ","signatureChunk":0}]}